[SPEAKER_03]: That's why you put yourself first and what you want first.

373
00:31:50,742 --> 00:31:52,664
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're talking about how to then...

374
00:31:54,742 --> 00:31:58,224
[SPEAKER_03]: I'm talking about right viewing of that relationship.

375
00:31:58,524 --> 00:32:01,606
[SPEAKER_03]: What is its core characteristic of that right viewing?

376
00:32:15,574 --> 00:32:21,978
[SPEAKER_00]: I would say, kind of, being complete in and of itself, as in those... as having no...

377
00:32:29,374 --> 00:32:31,155
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the entirety of it.

378
00:32:31,395 --> 00:32:33,435
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the entirety of it.

379
00:32:33,535 --> 00:32:45,398
[SPEAKER_03]: So, when you have two things that are complete to each other extent, how do they relate?

380
00:32:45,478 --> 00:32:46,678
[SPEAKER_03]: What is the relationship then?

381
00:32:46,718 --> 00:32:47,718
[SPEAKER_03]: How would you call it?

382
00:32:47,758 --> 00:32:48,458
[SPEAKER_01]: Simultaneous.

383
00:32:48,678 --> 00:32:49,038
[SPEAKER_03]: Exactly.

384
00:32:49,419 --> 00:32:50,779
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's the point.

385
00:32:51,659 --> 00:32:56,280
[SPEAKER_03]: The simultaneity of it... See, even if somebody, without a right view,

386
00:32:56,943 --> 00:33:05,909
[SPEAKER_03]: Where to start training, simultaneously knowing what they're attending, while they're attending it, it would bring them closer to the knowledge of that thing without needing to attempt it directly.

387
00:33:07,330 --> 00:33:10,972
[SPEAKER_03]: Because simultaneity of you and that thing is not something you can do.

388
00:33:11,333 --> 00:33:21,459
[SPEAKER_03]: Doesn't matter how much you emphasize your attending and your dealing and your movement and your reaction or whatever else, it remains unchanged.

389
00:33:21,579 --> 00:33:22,940
[SPEAKER_03]: It's all within that.

390
00:33:23,961 --> 00:33:25,462
[SPEAKER_03]: So, with this, this is.

391
00:33:25,867 --> 00:33:30,790
[SPEAKER_03]: And for as long that remains unchanging, that simultaneously is there.

392
00:33:30,810 --> 00:33:36,734
[SPEAKER_03]: And then you realize, once you recognize that simultaneity, that's why you cannot own things, because your ownership comes second to it.

393
00:33:37,055 --> 00:33:44,080
[SPEAKER_03]: It's just one of the things, one of the two things that are simultaneously dependent on each other, to the extent that they are.

394
00:33:44,640 --> 00:33:54,006
[SPEAKER_03]: Or try to own, or trying to possess, or trying to control, or acting upon, putting yourself first in regard to things that are simultaneously present,

395
00:33:55,394 --> 00:33:56,555
[SPEAKER_03]: It's the reason for dukkha.

396
00:33:57,476 --> 00:34:00,338
[SPEAKER_03]: That's why they are impermanent, because they are otherwise.

397
00:34:00,979 --> 00:34:18,753
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, and that's also... because if you see that that's simultaneous and the dependency of it, that you can't have the sense of, like, if you're... if it's the sense of me, for example, and this other thing... No, you have.

398
00:34:19,154 --> 00:34:21,035
[SPEAKER_00]: As in... but what... yeah, you have that.

399
00:34:21,055 --> 00:34:21,796
[SPEAKER_00]: You start with that.

400
00:34:21,876 --> 00:34:22,076
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

401
00:34:22,668 --> 00:34:27,754
[SPEAKER_00]: But you see them as simultaneously dependent.

402
00:34:27,814 --> 00:34:30,237
[SPEAKER_03]: It's literally kind of... That's what I meant by complete.

403
00:34:30,397 --> 00:34:30,637
[SPEAKER_03]: I know.

404
00:34:30,657 --> 00:34:31,558
[SPEAKER_03]: That's why I said go on.

405
00:34:31,939 --> 00:34:32,339
[SPEAKER_03]: Carry on.

406
00:34:32,399 --> 00:34:34,001
[SPEAKER_03]: Because that brings you closer to the point.

407
00:34:34,121 --> 00:34:39,207
[SPEAKER_03]: When there are two and there is no other option there, neither can be the one.

408
00:34:39,568 --> 00:34:39,968
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

409
00:34:40,028 --> 00:34:40,749
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's the point.

410
00:34:40,889 --> 00:34:43,332
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's why you do start with your sense of self.

411
00:34:44,233 --> 00:34:53,558
[SPEAKER_03]: You don't deny it, but by seeing it simultaneously dependent on something else that's not your sense of self, right there, to the same extent, it will cease to be yours.

412
00:34:53,899 --> 00:34:55,399
[SPEAKER_03]: It will still remain.

413
00:34:55,419 --> 00:35:00,042
[SPEAKER_03]: Nāmarūpa, viññāṇa, viññāṇa, nāmarūpa, dependence from bodhisattva still remains in the Nārāyaṇa.

414
00:35:00,842 --> 00:35:04,985
[SPEAKER_03]: What does not remain there is the ownership of either or both and so on.

415
00:35:06,158 --> 00:35:11,160
[SPEAKER_03]: So, exactly, on the level of the experience as a whole, not like me in this chair or something.

416
00:35:11,300 --> 00:35:13,441
[SPEAKER_03]: Although, if you're viewing it rightly, it can be anything.

417
00:35:13,481 --> 00:35:31,268
[SPEAKER_03]: But in the beginning, you need to start seeing it on the level of your experience as a whole, like your totality of your experience here and now, seated in this room, sense of I am, with... and then whatever, wherever your attention lands, whether it's a particular feeling, or a thought, or a memory, or

418
00:35:32,149 --> 00:35:34,270
[SPEAKER_03]: Or a pleasure or pain, doesn't matter.

419
00:35:34,290 --> 00:35:37,652
[SPEAKER_03]: Anything that you are relating to, there and then, to that extent.

420
00:35:39,953 --> 00:35:46,097
[SPEAKER_03]: And if you start seeing it as simultaneously enduring, as opposed to me dealing with this, then me dealing with that.

421
00:35:46,677 --> 00:35:49,759
[SPEAKER_03]: No, it's me dealing with this, while both are there to the same extent.

422
00:35:52,120 --> 00:35:56,062
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, a new me dealing with this, with something new, to the same extent.

423
00:35:56,262 --> 00:35:59,084
[SPEAKER_03]: Then, a new me dealing with something new, to the same extent.

424
00:36:00,244 --> 00:36:01,125
[SPEAKER_03]: Because that's what it really is.

425
00:36:03,454 --> 00:36:07,737
[SPEAKER_03]: And it's the recognition of that simultaneity that can bring you the closest to the right view.

426
00:36:09,258 --> 00:36:10,178
[SPEAKER_03]: And it's not something you can do.

427
00:36:11,019 --> 00:36:17,883
[SPEAKER_03]: You cannot possibly lay out the method of step-by-step method of the one, two, three, and then you see the simultaneity.

428
00:36:23,007 --> 00:36:31,553
[SPEAKER_03]: And that is pretty much the core of the the beginning that remains unchanged from the start till the end, till the completion of the practice.

429
00:36:33,577 --> 00:36:36,218
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the point is... Yeah.

430
00:36:36,398 --> 00:36:40,559
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the point is like... Do with this disease.

431
00:36:41,119 --> 00:36:41,959
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, exactly.

432
00:36:42,639 --> 00:36:47,840
[SPEAKER_03]: Because the actual principle of vipassana upādāna, regardless of its formulation in the suttas, always starts with this, this is.

433
00:36:49,281 --> 00:36:50,681
[SPEAKER_02]: It must mean sati and anhoti.

434
00:36:51,041 --> 00:36:51,361
[SPEAKER_03]: That's it.

435
00:36:51,901 --> 00:36:52,502
[SPEAKER_03]: Every single time.

436
00:36:53,182 --> 00:36:56,683
[SPEAKER_03]: Then, it can sometimes be eight factors, or twelve factors, or sixteen factors, and so on.

437
00:36:56,703 --> 00:36:58,723
[SPEAKER_03]: But you realize the principle remains unchanged.

438
00:37:00,396 --> 00:37:06,340
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you can't have the exemplification of the principle contradicting the principle, because then it's not the exemplification of the principle.

439
00:37:07,360 --> 00:37:13,404
[SPEAKER_03]: So, if the principle is with this, this is, then the exemplification of it has to be within that principle.

440
00:37:13,824 --> 00:37:16,446
[SPEAKER_03]: It's just basic logic.

441
00:37:17,187 --> 00:37:27,093
[SPEAKER_03]: But, again, given the whole commentarial tradition and Abhidhamma and the whole amount of of proliferated misleading information created on top of it,

442
00:37:28,162 --> 00:37:44,548
[SPEAKER_03]: Now, when people hear dependent origination, they have those those those wheel-of-life tankhas and stuff in their mind of how, you know, from rebirth, protest and stuff like that, fully ignoring or being completely unaware of the principle.

443
00:37:45,749 --> 00:37:53,232
[SPEAKER_03]: So, put the whole dependent origination aside, as a formulation, and just talk about yoniso, manasikāra,

444
00:37:54,588 --> 00:37:57,109
[SPEAKER_03]: Which is already the principle of patitsaṁupāda when seen rightly.

445
00:37:57,189 --> 00:38:07,252
[SPEAKER_03]: And somebody who develops yoniso manasikāra, learns how to attend to things simultaneously without attending to them directly, will then see how patitsaṁupāda formulation describes their very thing.

446
00:38:07,392 --> 00:38:20,717
[SPEAKER_03]: But, it's highly likely you would arrive at that thing through studying patitsaṁupāda formulation today, because of everything that happened in between, with commentarial and student studies and everything else, piles and piles and piles of pretty much misleading directions.

447
00:38:21,635 --> 00:38:25,696
[SPEAKER_01]: There's that other, that other sutta that says, where the Buddha gives us that basic description.

448
00:38:25,716 --> 00:38:26,376
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you see this?

449
00:38:27,057 --> 00:38:27,437
[SPEAKER_03]: Exactly.

450
00:38:27,677 --> 00:38:30,238
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, imagine we go... Do you see this as its condition?

451
00:38:30,258 --> 00:38:30,438
[SPEAKER_03]: Yep.

452
00:38:31,638 --> 00:38:36,520
[SPEAKER_03]: And then he asked the monks, the one who attends like this, can he doubt that?

453
00:38:36,580 --> 00:38:37,580
[SPEAKER_03]: Says, no, impossible.

454
00:38:38,400 --> 00:38:41,741
[SPEAKER_03]: The one who doesn't attend to it like this, can doubt arise?

455
00:38:41,841 --> 00:38:42,081
[SPEAKER_03]: Yes.

456
00:38:44,642 --> 00:38:45,602
[SPEAKER_01]: That's like the most...

457
00:38:46,163 --> 00:38:47,403
[SPEAKER_03]: It's as acute as it gets.

458
00:39:02,605 --> 00:39:05,746
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you see this as its condition?

459
00:39:06,726 --> 00:39:09,467
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you see the cessation of this condition?

460
00:39:11,107 --> 00:39:11,288
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

461
00:39:11,408 --> 00:39:19,370
[SPEAKER_03]: If this is not, would this be?

462
00:39:19,550 --> 00:39:24,251
[SPEAKER_01]: Is there uncertainty when there's doubt in regard to this?

463
00:39:24,291 --> 00:39:24,491
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

464
00:39:46,851 --> 00:40:14,099
[SPEAKER_03]: So, take your sense of self, however you might be feeling, in its entirety, here and now, and then discern, without abandoning, without attending something else, discern within that acute, concrete, living sense of self that you are, discern that something, in its core, that that self depends upon,

465
00:40:15,558 --> 00:40:16,679
[SPEAKER_03]: That it's not in your control.

466
00:40:21,181 --> 00:40:30,245
[SPEAKER_03]: So, bring that thing up to be simultaneously there, as an undermining condition for your sense of self.

467
00:40:30,845 --> 00:40:37,869
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, let it endure, and it's gonna... the mind will turn away from it, and then you repeat it, and then you repeat it, and then you repeat it.

468
00:40:45,349 --> 00:40:48,612
[SPEAKER_00]: And those same things, that's why... And you should be feeling it.

469
00:40:49,172 --> 00:40:49,513
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

470
00:40:49,613 --> 00:40:52,375
[SPEAKER_03]: It's not just on the level of, like, aha, see?

471
00:40:52,876 --> 00:40:53,256
[SPEAKER_03]: Okay.

472
00:40:54,117 --> 00:41:09,490
[SPEAKER_03]: If you get your sense of self feeling this or that, right here, right now, and while that's present, discern something in its heart that undermines it, yet it's not in your control, cannot be possessed, without abandoning this sense of self here and now.

473
00:41:11,055 --> 00:41:11,736
[SPEAKER_03]: You possess it.

474
00:41:11,936 --> 00:41:15,238
[SPEAKER_03]: When you make it a new thing, when you attend to it, it becomes your sense of self.

475
00:41:16,379 --> 00:41:18,841
[SPEAKER_03]: But now, you've fallen from that simultaneous recognition.

476
00:41:18,861 --> 00:41:28,548
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you're just moving from one to the other, from one to the other, maintaining your sense of ownership, because you're not undermining things through that simultaneity.

477
00:41:28,568 --> 00:41:34,792
[SPEAKER_03]: So, that's why it's important to recognize and not just attend to what... Oh, that's anicca, that's anicca, that's anicca.

478
00:41:34,992 --> 00:41:35,413
[SPEAKER_03]: No, no, no.

479
00:41:36,393 --> 00:41:37,434
[SPEAKER_03]: Take your sense of self,

480
00:41:38,175 --> 00:42:00,070
[SPEAKER_03]: However it feels, with its safety, with its ownership, with its implications, and without thinking about something else, discern, even if it's a vague thing, that there must be something simultaneously present, that actually your entirety of this experience of sense of self depends upon, yet that thing cannot be yours at the same time.

481
00:42:02,245 --> 00:42:06,008
[SPEAKER_03]: It can be yours after, but then something else won't be yours at the same time.

482
00:42:06,829 --> 00:42:08,270
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you're always undermined.

483
00:42:08,791 --> 00:42:11,633
[SPEAKER_03]: Wherever your attention rests, something else will undermine it.

484
00:42:12,194 --> 00:42:28,548
[SPEAKER_00]: But you could start, yeah, with just what makes you deeply anxious, this sort of deep existential anxiety that everybody experiences, and see what is on the base... what is that on the basis of?

485
00:42:30,428 --> 00:42:33,810
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, first, you have to start with the existential anxiety.

486
00:42:33,870 --> 00:42:37,972
[SPEAKER_03]: First, you have to not put yourself first that somebody's trying to get rid of it.

487
00:42:39,353 --> 00:42:41,454
[SPEAKER_03]: So, first, there's existential anxiety, okay?

488
00:42:42,074 --> 00:42:46,457
[SPEAKER_03]: Now, let me not try to get rid of it.

489
00:42:46,517 --> 00:42:49,398
[SPEAKER_03]: So, the existential anxiety is there, the sense of self is there.

490
00:42:49,718 --> 00:42:49,938
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

491
00:42:52,460 --> 00:42:54,341
[SPEAKER_03]: Let it endure.

492
00:42:55,261 --> 00:42:58,943
[SPEAKER_03]: Keep it, maintain it, simultaneously recognized.

493
00:43:02,908 --> 00:43:21,703
[SPEAKER_00]: And so then, like, that's why, I think you could say, like, the same contemplations that would, without the right view, be the source of the deepest fear or anxiety, would become the most pleasant thing to think about, really.

494
00:43:21,844 --> 00:43:24,105
[SPEAKER_00]: And it would bring you the closest to the right view.

495
00:43:24,125 --> 00:43:27,648
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it is the measure, then, of liberation.

496
00:43:29,350 --> 00:43:30,831
[SPEAKER_00]: There was actually that other citta,

497
00:43:31,193 --> 00:43:32,614
[SPEAKER_00]: It's something similar to that.

498
00:43:32,654 --> 00:43:38,519
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, I think it may be said... it would not be... if it were not...

499
00:43:38,559 --> 00:43:39,260
[SPEAKER_00]: It would not be for me.

500
00:43:39,280 --> 00:43:40,200
[SPEAKER_00]: It would not be for me.

501
00:43:40,541 --> 00:43:42,262
[SPEAKER_00]: It will not be... and it will not be mine.

502
00:43:42,602 --> 00:43:43,423
[SPEAKER_03]: It won't be mine.

503
00:43:43,683 --> 00:43:50,249
[SPEAKER_00]: And so he says this is terrifying to an ordinary person.

504
00:43:50,369 --> 00:43:50,529
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

505
00:43:50,749 --> 00:43:54,412
[SPEAKER_00]: And to an arahant or somebody or...

506
00:43:54,893 --> 00:43:55,973
[SPEAKER_00]: It's where the liberation is.

507
00:43:56,013 --> 00:43:57,275
[SPEAKER_00]: It is where the liberation is.

508
00:43:57,995 --> 00:44:00,137
[SPEAKER_03]: And it's terrifying not because it's genuinely

509
00:44:00,533 --> 00:44:01,474
[SPEAKER_03]: Terrifying in itself.

510
00:44:02,775 --> 00:44:04,196
[SPEAKER_03]: The cessation is not terrifying.

511
00:44:04,236 --> 00:44:11,582
[SPEAKER_03]: It's terrifying only if you still are unknowingly putting yourself first, which means your sense of ownership and control is there, maintained.

512
00:44:11,742 --> 00:44:17,147
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's why this is the the utmost contradiction to it, challenge to it.

513
00:44:17,207 --> 00:44:18,708
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's why it's the most frightening one.

514
00:44:19,429 --> 00:44:29,117
[SPEAKER_03]: So, if you have that, like, you know, most people wouldn't be able to hold it at the same time without losing perspective, without being overwhelmed by the fear or anxiety, if it's on that level.

515
00:44:29,929 --> 00:44:34,230
[SPEAKER_03]: But you can work up to it, and you work up to it by with what I just described.

516
00:44:34,270 --> 00:44:43,713
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you start by recognizing, I'm here, to this extent, such and such, my sense of self, sense of I am, real as such, no issue with it.

517
00:44:44,994 --> 00:44:58,338
[SPEAKER_03]: And with that experience as a whole, within that experience as a whole, without, again, trying to attend to a new thing, or fall into like a linear psychological explanation, discern, well, even just take your body present here, your beating heart,

518
00:44:58,865 --> 00:45:03,546
[SPEAKER_03]: Even there is a feeling, for that matter, that pretty much defines your sense of self there and then.

519
00:45:04,127 --> 00:45:06,927
[SPEAKER_03]: You realize you have no say in its simultaneous presence there.

520
00:45:07,928 --> 00:45:10,969
[SPEAKER_03]: You have no say in the simultaneous presence of the beating heart here.

521
00:45:11,029 --> 00:45:13,209
[SPEAKER_03]: Yet, my whole life depends on it.

522
00:45:14,290 --> 00:45:19,791
[SPEAKER_03]: This whole sense of self depends on this, I mean, living heart right here, as I'm thinking this.

523
00:45:20,652 --> 00:45:25,533
[SPEAKER_03]: If that changes, the slightest obstruction or something, none of this can remain.

524
00:45:27,367 --> 00:45:28,027
[SPEAKER_03]: What do you do next?

525
00:45:28,147 --> 00:45:28,568
[SPEAKER_03]: Repeat it.

526
00:45:30,108 --> 00:45:36,991
[SPEAKER_03]: And if it's... if you haven't forgotten it, if you haven't overly attended to it and made it into a new thing, then just... just let it endure there.

527
00:45:37,792 --> 00:45:39,072
[SPEAKER_03]: And obviously, you're gonna feel it.

528
00:45:40,373 --> 00:45:41,133
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's the measure.

529
00:45:41,193 --> 00:45:47,936
[SPEAKER_03]: When you're right attending, kind of first makes you feel things more, but then makes you unfeel them.

530
00:45:49,065 --> 00:45:49,465
[SPEAKER_03]: Same thing.

531
00:45:50,366 --> 00:45:51,527
[SPEAKER_03]: You're not doing anything extra.

532
00:45:52,188 --> 00:45:57,272
[SPEAKER_03]: Just through repeatedly attending them, the feelings were increasing, the fear, the pressure.

533
00:45:58,153 --> 00:45:59,134
[SPEAKER_03]: You didn't back down.

534
00:45:59,354 --> 00:46:06,960
[SPEAKER_03]: You kept repeatedly attending to it rightly, concurrently, yonisomanasikāra of it, indirectly, whichever way you want to translate it.

535
00:46:07,621 --> 00:46:13,326
[SPEAKER_03]: And now, those feelings are being unfelt, while nothing has changed.

536
00:46:14,767 --> 00:46:17,269
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, what is changing is the shift of the emphasis.

537
00:46:17,932 --> 00:46:22,503
[SPEAKER_03]: From over here, me, I am, onto not mine, not I am, over there.

538
00:46:23,244 --> 00:46:25,149
[SPEAKER_03]: It still remains that which was self-remained.

539
00:46:25,309 --> 00:46:26,412
[SPEAKER_03]: It's just not yours anymore.

